    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial For a Function Given Some Derivatives",
    "problem": "Let $f$ be a function with $f(3)=1,{f}^{′}(3)=1,{f}^{″}(3)=2$ and ${f}^{‴}(3)=3.$ Which of the following is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$",
    "worked_solution": "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(3)}{3!}(x-3)^{3} \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(3)}{6}(x-3)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =1+1(x-3)+\\frac{2}{2}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{3}{6}(x-3)^{3} \\\\  & =1+(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
